 Sure! Here is a TypeScript function using Axios to download all images created by the user `deniskropp` from the Civitai API:
```typescript
import axios from "axios";

const apiKey = "0be1d17f909493be1aa3b7f9224728f2";
const baseUrl = "https://api.civitai.com/api/v1";
const userName = "deniskropp";

async function downloadImages() {
  try {
    const response = await axios.get(`${baseUrl}/users/${userName}`, {
      headers: {
        Authorization: `Bearer ${apiKey}`
      }
    });

    if (response.data && Array.isArray(response.data.images)) {
      for (const image of response.data.images) {
        // You can customize this part based on how you want to handle and save the files
        console.log(`Downloading image: ${image.name}`);
        const imageResponse = await axios.get(`${image.file_url}?dl=true`, {
          responseType: "arraybuffer"
        });

        // Save the file as binary data or convert it into your preferred format before saving
        require("fs").writeFileSync(`./downloads/${image.name}.png`, imageResponse.data);
      }
    } else {
      throw new Error("Unexpected response structure");
    }
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("Error during image retrieval:", error.message);
  }
}

// Call the function
downloadImages();
```
This example assumes that you have installed Node.js with its package manager npm. To run this script, install the required packages via:
```bash
npm init -y
npm install axios fs
```
Afterward, create a file named `index.ts`, paste the code above, replace "./downloads/" in the `require('fs').writeFileSync` line with your desired directory path, then execute the following command to compile and run the program:
```bash
tsc index.ts && node index.js
```
